From 261048fca55b3597cb4b643be81eb899b61bef15 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:34:40 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] bt-hf: hand SCO to the audio framework and name the shipped
 dialer

Two independent gaps, both of which make an incoming HFP call go
nowhere.

The first is the audio path. The HF client role needs the software data
path and the audio framework has to own SCO, which is two vendor
properties: bluetooth.hfp.software_datapath.enabled and
bluetooth.sco.managed_by_audio. They only work together with the SCO
ports in bluetooth_audio_policy_configuration.xml and with the HF
client's own software data path, which comes from patches carried
outside this repository against packages/modules/Bluetooth; with a
subset, HFP ends up with neither the legacy setParameters route nor the
new session route. Measured on the board, 2026-08-27. Bluetooth starting
off on a fresh /data does not skip this: the HFP software-SCO setup
runs on every enable (bta_hf_client_api_enable), so turning Bluetooth
on still sets it up.

The SCO ports come with it. bluetooth_audio_policy_configuration.xml
gains the "hfp output" and "hfp input" mix ports at 8000, 16000 and
32000 Hz, mono, 16-bit -- the three rates CVSD, mSBC and LC3 negotiate
-- the BT SCO, BT SCO Headset and BT SCO Car Kit sinks, the BT SCO
Headset Mic source and their routes, with a header that says why the
rates are those. The mic port declares no gains: the port instance the
phone's connection creates carries none either, and the car audio
service's patch, carried outside this repository against
packages/services/Car, accepts a source without a gain controller.
The bridge itself was measured on the Raspberry Pi 4 (2026-09-19) with
a JOINT gain declared on that port; the port as declared here, without
one, has not been run on a board.
audio_policy_configuration.xml's note on the include says what the file
now declares.

The second is the incoming-call screen, and it is not a Bluetooth bug
at all. AOSP leaves config_defaultDialer at com.android.dialer, a
package no automotive build installs, and PermissionController resolves
the android.app.role.DIALER default holder from that string. The role
ends up with no holder, Telecom binds no InCallService, and the call
rings inside Telecom while the screen stays dark and silent -- no
screen, no ringtone, no call audio, while A2DP playback works.
car_product ships no overlay for it.

Diagnosed on a Raspberry Pi 5 board 2026-09-21; the evidence, including
the Telecom state machine reaching SET_RINGING with
combinedInCallServiceMap empty, the decisive
"doesConnectedDialerSupportRinging: no default dialer found" line, and
the runtime experiment that confirmed it, is in DEFAULT-DIALER.md. This
board has the same gap.

The string names no board and no hardware, so the overlay goes in this
repository's existing overlay root, which the product already names.

To measure rather than assume that the overlay lands -- it is a
framework-res overlay, and the root is a DEVICE_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS one:
    cmd role get-role-holders --user 10 android.app.role.DIALER
has to answer com.android.car.dialer.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending [automotive-grade-linux/Android_device_sodev_xenvm-cf]
